e('Both cost something on every single request, to protect against an event that may happen once a week.','Their costs and the frequency of deadlocks depend on the workload.','Unsupported frequency and universal per-request cost removed.')
e("It's how almost every relational database handles deadlock.",'PostgreSQL, InnoDB and SQL Server support this approach.','Unsupported market-wide prevalence narrowed.',pg+my+sql)
e("A database runs arbitrary SQL from many applications, and it can't force them to touch rows in a global order.",'Arbitrary SQL can discover rows in different orders; imposing one global order on every transaction is restrictive. Applications can still deliberately use consistent lock ordering.','Not a fundamental impossibility.',pg)
e('Recovery is **cheap and safe**. A transaction can be rolled back cleanly; that\'s what transactions are for.','Recovery is **supported and affordable for the workload**. Transactional database changes can be rolled back; rollback may be expensive, and external effects need separate handling.','Rollback neither universally cheap nor covers external state.',pg)
