e('Including the abort count, or the transaction\'s age, in the cost fixes it.','Increasing protection for repeated victims can mitigate this. A starvation-freedom guarantee also needs fair scheduling, finite transactions and a policy that eventually protects each retry.','Aging heuristic alone no unconditional progress proof.')
e('All three major engines detect deadlocks,','These three engines detect deadlocks,','Not an exhaustive engine ranking.')
e('**MySQL InnoDB** checks immediately when a transaction starts waiting (`innodb_deadlock_detect`, on by default).','**MySQL InnoDB** enables detection by default through `innodb_deadlock_detect`. Detection implementation and scheduling vary by version; it should not be treated as a zero-delay guarantee.', 'Remove unsupported immediate per-wait implementation contract.',my)
e('A lock timeout (`lock_timeout` in PostgreSQL, `innodb_lock_wait_timeout` in MySQL) also breaks deadlocks, by aborting anything that waits too long. It needs no graph, which is why distributed systems lean on it. The cost: it can\'t tell a deadlock from a slow holder, so you either wait a long time or abort innocent transactions.','A lock timeout ends an overlong wait without proving a cycle. PostgreSQL marks the current transaction or subtransaction failed. InnoDB normally rolls back only the timed-out statement, leaving the transaction and earlier locks active; explicitly roll back the transaction when abandoning/retrying the whole unit. Merely timing out a statement does not guarantee that every held lock is released. A timeout cannot distinguish deadlock from a slow holder.','Timeout is not universally full transaction rollback or deadlock recovery.',my+pg)
e('Your application must treat that error as **retryable** and rerun the *entire* transaction, not just the failed statement, because everything the transaction did has been rolled back.','If retrying is appropriate, roll back and rerun the *entire* logical transaction with a retry limit. The example assumes no enclosing transaction or savepoint and no irreversible effects in the retried work.','Savepoints and retry safety constrain blanket guidance.',ps+pg)
