e('def run_txn(conn, work, tries=5):\n    for n in range(tries):','def run_txn(conn, work, tries=5):\n    if conn.info.transaction_status != (\n        TransactionStatus.IDLE\n    ):\n        raise ValueError("idle conn required")\n    if tries < 1:\n        raise ValueError("tries must be > 0")\n    for n in range(tries):','Existing transaction would make transaction() a savepoint, not whole retry.',ps)
e('        except RETRY:\n            cap','        except RETRY:\n            if n + 1 == tries:\n                raise\n            cap','Preserve final database exception and avoid pointless final sleep.',ps)
e('    raise RuntimeError("too many retries")\n','', 'Final attempt rethrows original exception.',ps)
e('`conn.transaction()` rolls back if an exception escapes, so each attempt starts clean. Random back-off stops the same two transactions colliding again straight away.','Use a dedicated connection, initially idle (for example, an autocommit connection). `work` must not commit or roll back explicitly. Each context then owns a whole transaction, rolls back on error, and commits on successful exit. Jitter reduces repeated collisions; it does not guarantee progress.','Nested contexts and concurrent use invalidate wrapper guarantee.',ps)
e('A deterministic deadlock (two code paths that always lock in opposite order under load) will fail every time.','Opposite lock ordering can repeatedly deadlock when operations overlap, even if some retries succeed.','Order inversion is potential cycle not every execution.')
e('so it eventually becomes the oldest and can\'t starve.','so newer arrivals do not continually jump ahead. Eventual completion also assumes older transactions finish and scheduling/retries are fair.','Fairness assumptions needed.',sp)
e('- PostgreSQL checks after `deadlock_timeout` (1 s), InnoDB checks on every wait, SQL Server checks every 5 s; all abort one victim.','- PostgreSQL uses `deadlock_timeout` (default 1 s); InnoDB enables detection by default; SQL Server adapts its default 5 s checking interval. Victim rollback breaks detected cycles.','Version-aware summary; multiple victims possible.',pg+my+sql)
e('- Applications must catch deadlock errors and retry the whole transaction with back-off, keeping external side effects out of the retry loop.','- When retrying, restart the whole logical transaction with bounded back-off and safe handling of external effects.','Do not require blind retries.',ps)
f(1,'text',"Arbitrary SQL may acquire locks in different orders, while transactional database changes can be rolled back",'Global ordering not impossible.',pg,o=1)
f(1,'explanation','Correct. Detection supports diverse access orders; applications can still reduce deadlocks with consistent ordering. Transaction rollback is supported, although its cost varies.', 'Remove universal cheap undo log.',pg,o=1)
f(1,'workedExample','A globally enforced row order can restrict dynamically discovered SQL access patterns. Detection instead identifies a cycle and rolls back a victim using the engine\'s transaction machinery. This is attractive when the cost of occasional recovery is acceptable; applications should still keep transactions short and use consistent lock ordering where practical. External side effects are not automatically undone.','Remove unsupported incidence and universal undo implementation.',pg)
f(2,'explanation','A two-transaction cycle is sufficient; even a self-dependency can deadlock a non-reentrant resource. Three transactions are not required.','Unsupported most-common ranking.',th,o=1)
f(3,'prompt','Your Python service gets `psycopg.errors.DeadlockDetected` (SQLSTATE `40P01`) halfway through a top-level transaction that ran three `UPDATE`s. There are no savepoints or external side effects, and a bounded retry is allowed. What should it do?','Scope wholetransaction claim.',ps+pg)
