f(9,'explanation','A detector can abort one or more victims as needed. Duplicate emails here come from rerunning the same order after a database rollback, regardless of other victims.','One victim percycle not universal.',my,o=2)
f(10,'text','With detection on, maintaining and searching wait dependencies can itself become a bottleneck when many transactions queue on the same rows','No strict perwaitsearch assertion.',my,o=0)
f(10,'explanation','Correct. The InnoDB manual documents detector overhead under high contention; exact triggering and scheduling are version-dependent.','Primary source narrower than original.',my,o=0)
f(10,'text','With detection off, lock wait timeouts end overlong waits; applications must handle rollback and choose an appropriate timeout','Do not promise lockrelease/defaulttiming.',my,o=2)
f(10,'explanation','Correct. InnoDB normally rolls back only the waiting statement on timeout, so explicitly roll back when abandoning the entire transaction. Earlier locks otherwise remain held.','Critical timeout rollback distinction.',my,o=2)
f(10,'explanation','The waiting statement normally reaches its configured timeout, but its transaction can remain active and retain earlier locks. Application cleanup or whole-transaction timeout rollback is needed.','Timeout alone not full recovery.',my,o=1)
f(10,'workedExample','Detection precisely identifies wait cycles but can add contention overhead. With it disabled, choose lock wait timeouts based on the workload and handle timeout errors explicitly. InnoDB normally rolls back only the failed statement; a whole-unit retry should first roll back the transaction. A timeout is not proof of deadlock, and no universal few-second value is justified. Shorter transactions and consistent ordering can reduce contention.','Remove unsupported operator norms and immediate claims.',my)
f(11,'workedExample','Repeatedly selecting the cheapest victim can starve a small transaction. A policy can increase protection with age or prior abort count, while assuming finite competing transactions and fair retries. SQL Server offers explicit DEADLOCK_PRIORITY as another control, but HIGH is not immunity: another participant can have equal or higher priority, and a victim still has to be chosen.','HIGH doesnotmeanmustnotlose;agingconditional.',sql)
